WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.620
&gt;&gt; Hi. Hello everyone.

00:00:06.631 --> 00:00:09.530
Hi I'm [inaudible] .

00:00:09.531 --> 00:00:13.270
I'm a researcher here at the LTC.

00:00:13.271 --> 00:00:15.165
I'm their [inaudible] and

00:00:15.166 --> 00:00:17.670
I'm going to be chairing
the session this afternoon.

00:00:17.671 --> 00:00:21.360
We're going to have
three awesome talks

00:00:21.361 --> 00:00:23.610
and then we're
going to start with

00:00:23.611 --> 00:00:25.935
Kristen and she's
going to be talking

00:00:25.936 --> 00:00:29.200
about Adverbial Clausal Modifiers

00:00:29.201 --> 00:00:30.890
in the LinGO Grammar Matrix.

00:00:30.891 --> 00:00:32.580
So take it away.

00:00:32.581 --> 00:00:35.190
&gt;&gt; Okay. Hi, today
I'll be presenting

00:00:35.191 --> 00:00:37.830
an extension to
the LinGO Grammar matrix that

00:00:37.831 --> 00:00:39.870
will allow for
the automatic production of

00:00:39.871 --> 00:00:41.310
precision grammars that

00:00:41.311 --> 00:00:43.680
accommodate adverbial
clausal modifiers.

00:00:43.681 --> 00:00:46.440
So this is a little bit
less of an NLP talk,

00:00:46.441 --> 00:00:49.110
so I'm going to start by
giving you a background

00:00:49.111 --> 00:00:50.490
on precision grammars and

00:00:50.491 --> 00:00:52.380
the grammar matrix in particular,

00:00:52.381 --> 00:00:54.465
and then I'll
discuss the topology

00:00:54.466 --> 00:00:57.450
of clausal modifiers in
the world languages,

00:00:57.451 --> 00:00:59.490
introduce you briefly to

00:00:59.491 --> 00:01:01.605
our syntactic analysis
and then go through

00:01:01.606 --> 00:01:03.840
our development of the library

00:01:03.841 --> 00:01:06.750
and our evaluation
and error analysis.

00:01:06.751 --> 00:01:09.010
So precision grammars are

00:01:09.011 --> 00:01:10.360
bi-directional grammars that

00:01:10.361 --> 00:01:12.935
parse and generate sentences,

00:01:12.936 --> 00:01:15.430
but their strength
is that they produce

00:01:15.431 --> 00:01:18.520
very precise syntactic and
semantic representations.

00:01:18.521 --> 00:01:21.550
In our case, the syntactic
representations are in

00:01:21.551 --> 00:01:23.590
the HPSG formalism and

00:01:23.591 --> 00:01:27.285
the semantics are using
minimal recursion semantics.

00:01:27.286 --> 00:01:30.050
The most important thing
about these grammars is that

00:01:30.051 --> 00:01:32.225
they prioritize precision meaning

00:01:32.226 --> 00:01:35.270
we care about what
proportion of our parses are

00:01:35.271 --> 00:01:37.820
the right parse rather

00:01:37.821 --> 00:01:40.455
than being as concerned
with our overall coverage.

00:01:40.456 --> 00:01:42.020
Uses for these grammars

00:01:42.021 --> 00:01:45.560
include building grammars
for languages where

00:01:45.561 --> 00:01:47.150
we don't necessarily have

00:01:47.151 --> 00:01:51.035
enough data for
traditional NLP methods,

00:01:51.036 --> 00:01:53.585
but we do have
linguistic information

00:01:53.586 --> 00:01:55.085
that we can utilize.

00:01:55.086 --> 00:01:58.000
Linguists use these grammars
for hypothesis testing,

00:01:58.001 --> 00:02:01.310
checking their analyses over

00:02:01.311 --> 00:02:04.200
a corpus and these
are also useful

00:02:04.201 --> 00:02:07.470
for finding out what's
actually in your corpus.

00:02:07.471 --> 00:02:10.050
When you run your corpus
through one of these grammars,

00:02:10.051 --> 00:02:11.970
you might find a phenomenon

00:02:11.971 --> 00:02:14.100
that you didn't realize was there

00:02:14.101 --> 00:02:15.720
or you might actually find

00:02:15.721 --> 00:02:17.520
some inconsistencies either in

00:02:17.521 --> 00:02:19.764
your data or in your annotation.

00:02:19.765 --> 00:02:21.780
However, these grammars are

00:02:21.781 --> 00:02:25.015
very expensive and time
consuming to create.

00:02:25.016 --> 00:02:27.445
So, the LinGO Grammar Matrix is

00:02:27.446 --> 00:02:31.660
a starter kit for creating
the HPSG grammars.

00:02:31.661 --> 00:02:35.250
Basically it elicits
information from the user about

00:02:35.251 --> 00:02:38.855
the language and then
produces grammars from that.

00:02:38.856 --> 00:02:40.470
So a quick example.

00:02:40.471 --> 00:02:42.660
This is the word order page on

00:02:42.661 --> 00:02:45.240
the customization screen and

00:02:45.241 --> 00:02:46.275
it asks the user

00:02:46.276 --> 00:02:47.910
what's the basic word
order of the language;

00:02:47.911 --> 00:02:49.200
is the subject verb object,

00:02:49.201 --> 00:02:51.450
subject object verb and so forth?

00:02:51.451 --> 00:02:54.390
Do the determiners come
before or after the noun?

00:02:54.391 --> 00:02:55.770
Do auxiliaries come before or

00:02:55.771 --> 00:02:57.750
after their verbal complements?

00:02:57.751 --> 00:03:01.080
Then based on the user's
input on this page

00:03:01.081 --> 00:03:03.810
and analyses developed based

00:03:03.811 --> 00:03:05.550
on the typological literature,

00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:07.075
a precision grammar is

00:03:07.076 --> 00:03:10.210
output that can now
accommodate that phenomenon.

00:03:10.211 --> 00:03:12.420
Right now the grammar
matrix includes

00:03:12.421 --> 00:03:14.235
a wide variety of phenomenon

00:03:14.236 --> 00:03:16.530
including word order morphology,

00:03:16.531 --> 00:03:19.410
tense and aspect, but
previously it did

00:03:19.411 --> 00:03:22.485
not accommodate subordinate
clauses of any kind.

00:03:22.486 --> 00:03:24.990
So today we're
presenting a library

00:03:24.991 --> 00:03:28.140
for adverbial clausal modifiers.

00:03:28.141 --> 00:03:31.320
These are your
subordinate clauses

00:03:31.321 --> 00:03:34.830
that present
information like time,

00:03:34.831 --> 00:03:38.755
purpose, your basic while
because type clauses.

00:03:38.756 --> 00:03:42.060
In the world's languages
these manifest in

00:03:42.061 --> 00:03:45.630
a number of ways but follow
three basic patterns.

00:03:45.631 --> 00:03:48.750
Typically clausal
modifiers are marked by

00:03:48.751 --> 00:03:50.760
a free subordinating
morpheme and I'll

00:03:50.761 --> 00:03:53.265
just use the word
subordinator moving forward.

00:03:53.266 --> 00:03:55.650
This is a word like win or

00:03:55.651 --> 00:03:58.135
tow in the Japanese
example at the top.

00:03:58.136 --> 00:04:00.630
I can point to that.

00:04:00.631 --> 00:04:03.720
Another pattern is
subordinator pairs

00:04:03.721 --> 00:04:06.480
and we see these very
robustly in Mandarin.

00:04:06.481 --> 00:04:10.230
Such as the yinwei so ui pair,

00:04:10.231 --> 00:04:11.775
where you have this

00:04:11.776 --> 00:04:15.060
because subordinator in
the subordinate clause,

00:04:15.061 --> 00:04:16.260
but then in the matrix
clause you have

00:04:16.261 --> 00:04:17.550
to have this additional adverb.

00:04:17.551 --> 00:04:20.850
These are also really
common in if-then clauses

00:04:20.851 --> 00:04:22.990
cross-linguistically
and it's easier

00:04:22.991 --> 00:04:24.870
to think of an English example.

00:04:24.871 --> 00:04:27.970
Finally, sometimes
subordinate clauses

00:04:27.971 --> 00:04:30.685
are marked just with
verbal morphology.

00:04:30.686 --> 00:04:34.450
So the purposive suffix right
here in this example from

00:04:34.451 --> 00:04:37.330
Louise signo marks that
subordinate clause

00:04:37.331 --> 00:04:41.130
as a purposive clause.

00:04:41.131 --> 00:04:43.030
In addition to
these basic patterns,

00:04:43.031 --> 00:04:46.150
we also see a very wide range

00:04:46.151 --> 00:04:49.315
of variation within
these clauses and

00:04:49.316 --> 00:04:52.270
any given language will
probably be marked by

00:04:52.271 --> 00:04:55.565
a handful of different strategies

00:04:55.566 --> 00:04:57.750
that utilize
these characteristics.

00:04:57.751 --> 00:04:59.820
So these include the position of

00:04:59.821 --> 00:05:00.960
the clausal modifier whether it's

00:05:00.961 --> 00:05:03.200
before or after the main clause,

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:07.190
whether or not there's a pair,

00:05:07.191 --> 00:05:09.270
whether there's
additional verbal morphology,

00:05:09.271 --> 00:05:11.220
so you might have
a subordinator and then also a

00:05:11.221 --> 00:05:13.605
non-finite verb in the
subordinate clause.

00:05:13.606 --> 00:05:15.720
Sometimes the clause
is nominalized

00:05:15.721 --> 00:05:17.715
or the subject is shared,

00:05:17.716 --> 00:05:19.260
so it's unexpressed in

00:05:19.261 --> 00:05:22.850
the subordinate clause but
expressed in the main clause.

00:05:22.851 --> 00:05:24.750
So this is an example like;

00:05:24.751 --> 00:05:26.900
I went to the store to buy bread,

00:05:26.901 --> 00:05:28.530
where I is the subject of

00:05:28.531 --> 00:05:32.080
both clauses but unexpressed
in the purposive clause.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:35.880
So in order to

00:05:35.881 --> 00:05:38.130
account for the space and

00:05:38.131 --> 00:05:40.220
analyze this within
the grammar matrix,

00:05:40.221 --> 00:05:45.040
we introduce two lexical types
and two unary rules.

00:05:45.041 --> 00:05:46.260
And abstracting away from

00:05:46.261 --> 00:05:49.605
the nitty-gritty HPSG
on this slide,

00:05:49.606 --> 00:05:52.080
basically what we've
done is we've created

00:05:52.081 --> 00:05:56.070
four supertypes that account

00:05:56.071 --> 00:05:58.500
for the basics of
each strategy whether

00:05:58.501 --> 00:06:02.625
it's a subordinator or
morphological subordination.

00:06:02.626 --> 00:06:04.960
Then onto the supertypes,

00:06:04.961 --> 00:06:07.405
we can add new features
in the subtypes.

00:06:07.406 --> 00:06:09.975
So to account for

00:06:09.976 --> 00:06:12.755
all of these characteristics
on this slide,

00:06:12.756 --> 00:06:16.950
we add feature
definitions that we

00:06:16.951 --> 00:06:18.810
can put into the subtypes

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:21.015
for each subordinate
clause strategy.

00:06:21.016 --> 00:06:24.645
So for example, we use
the posttype feature,

00:06:24.646 --> 00:06:27.870
which is just a Boolean feature
to constrain whether

00:06:27.871 --> 00:06:29.385
the clausal modifier goes

00:06:29.386 --> 00:06:31.950
before or after the main clause.

00:06:31.951 --> 00:06:35.290
And if it's free to
go in either place,

00:06:35.291 --> 00:06:37.730
then really that underspecified.

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:42.840
In our development, we use
two sets of development data.

00:06:42.841 --> 00:06:46.350
Sorry about that.

00:06:46.351 --> 00:06:48.430
A quick example of how we

00:06:48.431 --> 00:06:53.420
map those features onto
the customization page for

00:06:53.421 --> 00:06:55.220
the user is that we use

00:06:55.221 --> 00:06:57.890
an iterator that allows

00:06:57.891 --> 00:06:59.480
a user to define as many clausal

00:06:59.481 --> 00:07:01.160
modifier strategies as they want.

00:07:01.161 --> 00:07:03.560
So they can have the purpose
of strategy that just

00:07:03.561 --> 00:07:07.010
uses the drop subject in the
non-finite and to buy bread,

00:07:07.011 --> 00:07:08.660
and they can use
a different strategy

00:07:08.661 --> 00:07:11.390
for a because clause
which is marked by

00:07:11.391 --> 00:07:14.120
a subordinator and they're

00:07:14.121 --> 00:07:17.370
free to fill in as many
strategies as they need.

00:07:17.371 --> 00:07:21.345
So when developing, we use
two sets of development data.

00:07:21.346 --> 00:07:24.050
Our first stage is
pseudo languages.

00:07:24.051 --> 00:07:26.660
These are languages with

00:07:26.661 --> 00:07:28.190
a minimal lexicon that are

00:07:28.191 --> 00:07:30.835
designed to capture
the typological space.

00:07:30.836 --> 00:07:33.770
So we'll just use
a simple language

00:07:33.771 --> 00:07:35.180
where the nouns are
called noun one,

00:07:35.181 --> 00:07:37.615
noun two and the verbs are
called verb one, verb two.

00:07:37.616 --> 00:07:41.090
Then we captured
the typological space as best we

00:07:41.091 --> 00:07:46.725
can by pairing each subordinator
type with each feature.

00:07:46.726 --> 00:07:48.945
So going back to
the previous slide,

00:07:48.946 --> 00:07:51.660
we have all of these
feature values that are

00:07:51.661 --> 00:07:54.825
possible on
the subordinator types

00:07:54.826 --> 00:07:57.570
and we'll test this add

00:07:57.571 --> 00:07:59.595
position subordinator
lexical type

00:07:59.596 --> 00:08:01.605
with one of the post
add features.

00:08:01.606 --> 00:08:03.960
But if we were to actually
test all of them,

00:08:03.961 --> 00:08:07.140
we'd have a 1008 tests languages

00:08:07.141 --> 00:08:08.820
and that would be really
cumbersome because these aren't

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:11.710
just test instances these
are full languages.

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:14.839
So, the end of this stage,

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:16.675
we expect four coverage.

00:08:16.676 --> 00:08:20.420
We should be able to capture
the typological space by

00:08:20.421 --> 00:08:24.855
accounting for every possible
combination of features.

00:08:24.856 --> 00:08:28.100
However, we don't test
each feature value,

00:08:28.101 --> 00:08:29.920
just every feature type.

00:08:29.921 --> 00:08:33.575
After that, our second
development set

00:08:33.576 --> 00:08:35.095
is illustrative languages,

00:08:35.096 --> 00:08:37.070
and at this point we actually

00:08:37.071 --> 00:08:39.645
get involved with
natural language.

00:08:39.646 --> 00:08:42.370
It's a bit messier
because we're testing

00:08:42.371 --> 00:08:44.970
interactions that actually
occur in real languages,

00:08:44.971 --> 00:08:47.500
such as subject dropping
in the language,

00:08:47.501 --> 00:08:49.180
various word order patterns,

00:08:49.181 --> 00:08:50.920
and these interacting phenomena

00:08:50.921 --> 00:08:52.500
can cause complications
that weren't

00:08:52.501 --> 00:08:54.860
previously tested in the
pseudo language evaluation

00:08:54.861 --> 00:08:57.430
or pseudo language testing.

00:08:57.431 --> 00:09:01.560
So, we used four
real languages that

00:09:01.561 --> 00:09:04.285
were selected specifically to

00:09:04.286 --> 00:09:06.290
illustrate a
particular phenomena.

00:09:06.291 --> 00:09:08.910
So, we use Mandarin to illustrate

00:09:08.911 --> 00:09:12.680
the subordinator pair that
I've discussed previously.

00:09:12.681 --> 00:09:16.470
Rukai requires that subordinate
clauses be nominalized.

00:09:16.471 --> 00:09:20.850
German is a v2 language,
verb second language,

00:09:20.851 --> 00:09:22.670
that requires verb
final word order

00:09:22.671 --> 00:09:24.020
and subordinate clauses,

00:09:24.021 --> 00:09:25.720
so we tested that.

00:09:25.721 --> 00:09:29.170
Then, Wambaya is
a language that marks

00:09:29.171 --> 00:09:31.295
its subordinate clauses
morphologically,

00:09:31.296 --> 00:09:32.450
we tested that as well.

00:09:32.451 --> 00:09:34.220
Now at this stage,

00:09:34.221 --> 00:09:35.930
we discovered one phenomena

00:09:35.931 --> 00:09:37.650
that's not captured
in our library,

00:09:37.651 --> 00:09:39.445
and probably shouldn't be,

00:09:39.446 --> 00:09:42.040
and this is that subordinate
clauses actually have

00:09:42.041 --> 00:09:47.975
a different case frame on
the objects in those clauses,

00:09:47.976 --> 00:09:51.530
so a nominative accusative
language will actually

00:09:51.531 --> 00:09:56.430
have dative case in the
subordinate clause.

00:09:56.431 --> 00:09:58.050
So, we decided that
this should actually

00:09:58.051 --> 00:10:00.510
be treated separately as

00:10:00.511 --> 00:10:02.250
a valence change in operation in

00:10:02.251 --> 00:10:05.010
another library that we
set aside for future work.

00:10:05.011 --> 00:10:08.040
So, we don't expect
four coverage here,

00:10:08.041 --> 00:10:11.770
as those examples aren't
expected to parse.

00:10:11.771 --> 00:10:14.765
So, finally, move
on to evaluation,

00:10:14.766 --> 00:10:19.540
and at this stage we test
the typological diversity and

00:10:19.541 --> 00:10:22.105
reliability of our system

00:10:22.106 --> 00:10:24.630
by testing on five
held out languages.

00:10:24.631 --> 00:10:26.030
This is not just held out data,

00:10:26.031 --> 00:10:27.660
but these are languages
that we did not

00:10:27.661 --> 00:10:30.165
consider at all
during development.

00:10:30.166 --> 00:10:32.420
The way we construct
our test suites and

00:10:32.421 --> 00:10:34.760
grammars is by reading
the descriptive grammars

00:10:34.761 --> 00:10:37.880
written by field linguists that

00:10:37.881 --> 00:10:41.540
have a lot of very detailed data
and prose descriptions,

00:10:41.541 --> 00:10:43.935
and we develop
our test suites from that.

00:10:43.936 --> 00:10:49.280
These languages were
selected in a random manner,

00:10:49.281 --> 00:10:50.990
but required to be genetically

00:10:50.991 --> 00:10:52.410
and geographically diverse.

00:10:52.411 --> 00:10:54.080
So, if I pulled

00:10:54.081 --> 00:10:56.415
two Indo-European languages
off the shelf,

00:10:56.416 --> 00:10:58.245
I would put them back

00:10:58.246 --> 00:11:01.845
until we represent five distinct
language families here.

00:11:01.846 --> 00:11:05.020
We achieved 88
percent coverage and

00:11:05.021 --> 00:11:08.005
two percent over-generation
at this point.

00:11:08.006 --> 00:11:10.090
That two percent
over-generation comes from

00:11:10.091 --> 00:11:12.735
just one ungrammatical
example parsing,

00:11:12.736 --> 00:11:16.115
and that really shows
our fidelity to precision here.

00:11:16.116 --> 00:11:19.945
As for our error analysis,

00:11:19.946 --> 00:11:22.120
this actually tells
us quite a bit about

00:11:22.121 --> 00:11:25.655
the library and about the
typology of clausal modifiers.

00:11:25.656 --> 00:11:28.010
So, I've broken this
into two categories,

00:11:28.011 --> 00:11:30.815
In Scope phenomena and
Out of Scope phenomena.

00:11:30.816 --> 00:11:33.910
We had one sentence
fell to parse in

00:11:33.911 --> 00:11:37.080
Moseten due to a constraint

00:11:37.081 --> 00:11:39.470
in the supertype that
shouldn't have been there,

00:11:39.471 --> 00:11:41.245
should have been
added in the subtype,

00:11:41.246 --> 00:11:44.050
so that's a bug and it's fixed.

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:48.480
Next, we found a bug in the
nominalized clauses library,

00:11:48.481 --> 00:11:51.920
where nominalized clauses had

00:11:51.921 --> 00:11:55.315
a special head subject rule
to allow for that attachment,

00:11:55.316 --> 00:11:57.935
but did not account
for subject dropping.

00:11:57.936 --> 00:12:00.000
Meaning, if a nominalized
clause did not have

00:12:00.001 --> 00:12:02.345
an overt subject,
that's going to parse,

00:12:02.346 --> 00:12:04.755
and it turns out that
it's possible in Basque,

00:12:04.756 --> 00:12:08.330
so we created a rule
and inserted that into

00:12:08.331 --> 00:12:09.950
the grammar to make
sure that that sentence

00:12:09.951 --> 00:12:12.610
would parse if
that rule was there.

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:16.605
Next we get to Out
of Scope Phenomena,

00:12:16.606 --> 00:12:18.240
and this is really
the interesting part

00:12:18.241 --> 00:12:19.300
of the error analysis,

00:12:19.301 --> 00:12:20.910
because we see what didn't

00:12:20.911 --> 00:12:22.935
come up in the
typological literature

00:12:22.936 --> 00:12:26.990
that turns out is out there
in the world's languages.

00:12:26.991 --> 00:12:28.700
Our first error came from

00:12:28.701 --> 00:12:31.884
the same case change issue
that we saw in Wambaya,

00:12:31.885 --> 00:12:34.675
and so I have an
example for you here.

00:12:34.676 --> 00:12:39.560
In this language,
third-person subjects are

00:12:39.561 --> 00:12:41.460
marked with accusative
case instead of

00:12:41.461 --> 00:12:44.330
nominative case in the
subordinate clause,

00:12:44.331 --> 00:12:45.860
and so you see that here.

00:12:45.861 --> 00:12:47.715
So, this wasn't accounted for,

00:12:47.716 --> 00:12:49.645
and we had one sentence

00:12:49.646 --> 00:12:51.510
that is grammatical
fail to parse,

00:12:51.511 --> 00:12:54.620
and then one ungrammatical
sentence parse,

00:12:54.621 --> 00:12:57.790
and that's what led to
our two percent over-generation.

00:12:57.970 --> 00:13:01.980
The next thing we
found in Ma'di is

00:13:01.981 --> 00:13:06.495
that subordinators actually
can attach to a verb.

00:13:06.496 --> 00:13:08.640
So, in the typological
literature,

00:13:08.641 --> 00:13:10.745
we saw the subordinators

00:13:10.746 --> 00:13:12.770
attaching either
to the sentence on

00:13:12.771 --> 00:13:15.240
the outside of the clause or to

00:13:15.241 --> 00:13:18.190
the verb phrase intervening
between a subject and verb.

00:13:18.191 --> 00:13:19.640
But we never saw it intervening

00:13:19.641 --> 00:13:21.130
between the verb and its object.

00:13:21.131 --> 00:13:22.665
In fact, in Ma'di,

00:13:22.666 --> 00:13:23.990
it's possible in the sentence,

00:13:23.991 --> 00:13:26.840
"If Opi brings beer, he
should put it on the shelf."

00:13:26.841 --> 00:13:29.755
'If' can intervene between
'bring' and 'beer'.

00:13:29.756 --> 00:13:31.500
So, this is easy enough
to add now that we

00:13:31.501 --> 00:13:34.090
know that it's possible
in the world's languages,

00:13:34.091 --> 00:13:36.530
and that'll be part
of our future work.

00:13:36.531 --> 00:13:42.830
Finally, we found in
Lavukaleve that the same pair,

00:13:42.831 --> 00:13:44.260
adverb phenomena that we see in

00:13:44.261 --> 00:13:47.040
phenomena or that you
see in Mandarin where

00:13:47.041 --> 00:13:49.000
you have a subordinator in

00:13:49.001 --> 00:13:52.290
the clausal modifier and
an adverb in the main clause,

00:13:52.291 --> 00:13:54.290
can occur where you have

00:13:54.291 --> 00:13:56.850
a morphologically marked
subordinate clause,

00:13:56.851 --> 00:13:59.570
and you still need an adverb
and the main clause.

00:13:59.571 --> 00:14:02.570
So, there was
only a brief description

00:14:02.571 --> 00:14:04.400
of this in the prose
and one example.

00:14:04.401 --> 00:14:05.820
So, what we would like to do is

00:14:05.821 --> 00:14:08.810
some further investigation in
the typological literature,

00:14:08.811 --> 00:14:10.200
as well as in that language

00:14:10.201 --> 00:14:11.730
before adding
this functionality to

00:14:11.731 --> 00:14:14.990
the library just to make sure.

00:14:14.991 --> 00:14:16.100
So, what we've done today

00:14:16.101 --> 00:14:18.730
is we'd introduced
a new library that now

00:14:18.731 --> 00:14:20.340
accommodates one type of

00:14:20.341 --> 00:14:22.500
subordinate clause in
the grammar matrix,

00:14:22.501 --> 00:14:24.180
so that these precision grammars

00:14:24.181 --> 00:14:26.050
can be automatically produced.

00:14:26.051 --> 00:14:28.400
Are there any questions?

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:39.035
I don't know who's.

00:14:39.036 --> 00:14:41.450
&gt;&gt; What do the
grammars look like,

00:14:41.451 --> 00:14:46.295
is it like for
structure rules or explore?

00:14:46.296 --> 00:14:47.750
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, the question is what

00:14:47.751 --> 00:14:49.055
do the grammars look like?

00:14:49.056 --> 00:14:50.740
So, we're using the head-driven

00:14:50.741 --> 00:14:52.535
phrase structure
grammar formalism,

00:14:52.536 --> 00:14:58.225
and this is a lexically-driven
syntactic framework,

00:14:58.226 --> 00:14:59.990
and we have a combination of

00:14:59.991 --> 00:15:03.270
lexical entries and
phrase structure rules. Yeah.

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:07.280
&gt;&gt; What does that bring
you up to for how much

00:15:07.281 --> 00:15:10.680
of English prose are
you able to parse now,

00:15:10.681 --> 00:15:13.400
what rules are you still
missing and how do

00:15:13.401 --> 00:15:14.500
you do all of it and

00:15:14.501 --> 00:15:16.305
just some other languages
you don't do?

00:15:16.306 --> 00:15:19.430
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.
So, the grammar matrix

00:15:19.431 --> 00:15:22.430
actually grew out of the
English resource grammar,

00:15:22.431 --> 00:15:25.690
which covers 96 percent of

00:15:25.691 --> 00:15:30.110
well-edited English
text, maybe it's 94.

00:15:30.111 --> 00:15:33.355
So that's a very large grammar.

00:15:33.356 --> 00:15:40.220
We haven't actually
tested this on English,

00:15:40.221 --> 00:15:42.220
like the grammar matrix
outputs on English,

00:15:42.221 --> 00:15:44.285
we are still missing a
number of phenomena.

00:15:44.286 --> 00:15:47.970
A library for clausal compliment
has just been added,

00:15:47.971 --> 00:15:50.240
but there's still
more to be done.

00:15:50.241 --> 00:15:51.500
We don't have relative clauses,

00:15:51.501 --> 00:15:53.480
we don't have WH questions,

00:15:53.481 --> 00:15:57.110
so still wouldn't be
quite close to there.

00:15:57.111 --> 00:15:58.440
But that work has been done

00:15:58.441 --> 00:15:59.830
on the individual language level.

00:15:59.831 --> 00:16:06.110
Other questions?

00:16:06.111 --> 00:16:08.380
Yes.

00:16:08.381 --> 00:16:11.690
&gt;&gt; So, one question
about what is left to

00:16:11.691 --> 00:16:14.960
do for any other world languages?

00:16:14.961 --> 00:16:16.325
You just did the

00:16:16.326 --> 00:16:18.800
adverbial clauses oris there
anything that's missing?

00:16:18.801 --> 00:16:20.450
&gt;&gt; Yeah. In terms of phenomena

00:16:20.451 --> 00:16:22.665
that are not yet in
the grammar matrix,

00:16:22.666 --> 00:16:24.070
we're still missing quite a bit.

00:16:24.071 --> 00:16:26.920
So, what I would recommend you

00:16:26.921 --> 00:16:30.220
do is go to the grammar matrix
page and see what's there,

00:16:30.221 --> 00:16:34.160
and then you should be able
to figure out what's not.

00:16:34.161 --> 00:16:36.390
But there are lots
of new phenomena,

00:16:36.391 --> 00:16:39.830
relative clauses, I think
it's a next direction.

00:16:39.831 --> 00:16:42.750
Adverbs themselves,
not adverbial clauses,

00:16:42.751 --> 00:16:45.050
but just adverbs haven't
been implemented yet.

00:16:45.051 --> 00:16:47.250
Yeah.

00:16:47.251 --> 00:16:49.450
Right.

00:16:49.451 --> 00:16:57.040
&gt;&gt; If you can just give her
an applause. Thank you.

00:16:57.041 --> 00:16:58.520
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:16:59.110 --> 00:17:01.870
&gt;&gt; Michael is going
to be talking about

00:17:01.871 --> 00:17:05.330
SimpleQuestions Nearly Solved and

00:17:05.331 --> 00:17:07.775
new unbounded in
baseline approach.

00:17:07.776 --> 00:17:10.510
It's a joint work with
Luke Zettlemoyer.

00:17:10.511 --> 00:17:13.180
Take it.

00:17:13.181 --> 00:17:15.190
&gt;&gt; Hi, there. My name is

00:17:15.191 --> 00:17:18.215
Michael and I'm
presenting as said,

00:17:18.216 --> 00:17:21.175
SimpleQuestions
Nearly Solved, this was

00:17:21.176 --> 00:17:22.350
undergraduate research that I

00:17:22.351 --> 00:17:26.260
did at the Paul G. Allen School.

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:32.235
To give you first
a brief overview of my talk,

00:17:32.236 --> 00:17:35.240
first we're going
to introduce you to

00:17:35.241 --> 00:17:38.600
the widely used
SimpleQuestions benchmark,

00:17:38.601 --> 00:17:41.015
and then we're going to go over

00:17:41.016 --> 00:17:43.235
the contributions
this paper has made.

00:17:43.236 --> 00:17:45.190
Onto that benchmark first,

00:17:45.191 --> 00:17:47.840
talking about that we
actually found that there

00:17:47.841 --> 00:17:50.540
is an upper bound
on this benchmark,

00:17:50.541 --> 00:17:54.155
and then introducing
a simple baseline method

00:17:54.156 --> 00:17:57.150
that actually get state
of the art results.

00:17:57.151 --> 00:17:59.690
Then, we'll conclude
that we found

00:17:59.691 --> 00:18:02.635
that through our analysis
our upper bound it's loose,

00:18:02.636 --> 00:18:07.160
and that the SimpleQuestions
dataset is nearly solved.

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:12.425
So, first, some background,

00:18:12.426 --> 00:18:14.440
what is a simple question?

00:18:14.441 --> 00:18:17.020
So, for example, who

00:18:17.021 --> 00:18:20.085
wrote Gulliver's Travels
is a simple question.

00:18:20.086 --> 00:18:22.800
The reason it's a simple
questions because it has

00:18:22.801 --> 00:18:25.134
one relation, one subject,

00:18:25.135 --> 00:18:28.200
and a simple question
stands for a

00:18:28.201 --> 00:18:32.450
single-relation, single-subject
factoid question.

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:36.205
Why are these questions
interesting to look at?

00:18:36.206 --> 00:18:40.310
Well, they appear often online,

00:18:40.311 --> 00:18:43.560
specifically Microsoft has them

00:18:43.561 --> 00:18:44.990
a lot in their query logs,

00:18:44.991 --> 00:18:47.314
we see them popping
up in Wiki answers,

00:18:47.315 --> 00:18:51.240
and they're commonly used
that voice assistance.

00:18:51.400 --> 00:18:54.330
Although they're
very commonly used.

00:18:54.331 --> 00:18:58.100
The most popular and largest
benchmark by a magnitude,

00:18:58.101 --> 00:19:02.330
the state of the art results
on it are only 77 percent.

00:19:04.240 --> 00:19:08.490
Okay. So, a little bit
more background.

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:12.900
The SimpleQuestions benchmark is

00:19:12.901 --> 00:19:16.415
the largest
SimpleQuestions dataset

00:19:16.416 --> 00:19:18.990
of a 108 thousand examples.

00:19:18.991 --> 00:19:21.990
Each example has a question that

00:19:21.991 --> 00:19:25.830
is labeled of a subject,
relation, and object.

00:19:25.831 --> 00:19:29.820
It has a back-end
Freebase knowledge graph,

00:19:29.821 --> 00:19:33.070
which is just a store facts.

00:19:33.860 --> 00:19:37.660
Then, bringing up an example
for that, for example,

00:19:37.661 --> 00:19:40.840
here is the relation and this
is the Freebase relation

00:19:40.841 --> 00:19:44.395
for who wrote in this context.

00:19:44.396 --> 00:19:47.740
For Gulliver's Travels,
the Freebase object has

00:19:47.741 --> 00:19:52.200
an MID in an alias
Gulliver's Travels.

00:19:53.830 --> 00:19:56.350
Okay. So the first contribution's

00:19:56.351 --> 00:19:58.475
introducing an upper bound.

00:19:58.476 --> 00:20:03.620
So, the example and
what's really driving

00:20:03.621 --> 00:20:05.235
this work is that there's

00:20:05.236 --> 00:20:06.830
fundamental contradictions in

00:20:06.831 --> 00:20:08.825
the SimpleQuestions dataset.

00:20:08.826 --> 00:20:11.245
For example, who wrote
Gulliver's travels

00:20:11.246 --> 00:20:13.330
is referring to
the TV miniseries,

00:20:13.331 --> 00:20:15.540
Gulliver's Travels, while
name a character from

00:20:15.541 --> 00:20:17.020
Gulliver's Travels is referring

00:20:17.021 --> 00:20:19.250
to Gulliver's Travels book.

00:20:19.251 --> 00:20:21.650
That shows that
the linguistic signal on

00:20:21.651 --> 00:20:24.055
these questions does
not provide or provides

00:20:24.056 --> 00:20:26.670
equal evidence for if being

00:20:26.671 --> 00:20:28.420
any other miniseries or a book in

00:20:28.421 --> 00:20:30.480
both cases, it's not clear.

00:20:30.481 --> 00:20:32.230
Okay.

00:20:32.231 --> 00:20:38.660
So, how do we quantify this
and get to an upper bound?

00:20:38.661 --> 00:20:46.575
So reminder, this is the data
that we're dealing with.

00:20:46.576 --> 00:20:51.125
So the first thing we do is
we take a look at the alias,

00:20:51.126 --> 00:20:52.515
come up with the list of

00:20:52.516 --> 00:20:55.560
all Gulliver's Travels
in Freebase.

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:59.820
Similarly, we take out
Gulliver's Travels from

00:20:59.821 --> 00:21:03.845
the question and we create
an abstract relation.

00:21:03.846 --> 00:21:05.870
In SimpleQuestions,

00:21:05.871 --> 00:21:10.070
who wrote co-occurs
with book written by,

00:21:10.071 --> 00:21:15.010
film written by, film story
by, and other relations.

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:20.140
We can take a cross product
of these and these

00:21:20.141 --> 00:21:22.100
are all possible interpretations

00:21:22.101 --> 00:21:24.080
of the question that
we looked at before,

00:21:24.081 --> 00:21:25.840
though some of them
don't make sense.

00:21:25.841 --> 00:21:28.180
For example, for
Gulliver's Travels of the book,

00:21:28.181 --> 00:21:31.560
you wouldn't have
the relation film.

00:21:31.561 --> 00:21:34.615
So we just do a filter on them on

00:21:34.616 --> 00:21:37.405
which ones we actually
find in Freebase.

00:21:37.406 --> 00:21:40.015
From that, we come up with a list

00:21:40.016 --> 00:21:42.390
of multiple correct
interpretations of who wrote

00:21:42.391 --> 00:21:44.605
Gulliver's Travels
for which we have

00:21:44.606 --> 00:21:47.250
equal linguistic evidence for

00:21:47.251 --> 00:21:48.780
each one of this being correct,

00:21:48.781 --> 00:21:50.620
yet we only have one ground truth

00:21:50.621 --> 00:21:53.200
for example which
is number three.

00:21:54.430 --> 00:21:56.705
Okay. So our results,

00:21:56.706 --> 00:22:00.650
we find that on
this widely used benchmark,

00:22:00.651 --> 00:22:04.090
33.9 percent of
the questions have

00:22:04.091 --> 00:22:05.915
multiple correct interpretations

00:22:05.916 --> 00:22:08.670
and therefore unanswerable.

00:22:10.920 --> 00:22:13.970
So, in computing the upper bound,

00:22:13.971 --> 00:22:18.290
we find that not all subject
relations are equal.

00:22:18.610 --> 00:22:21.545
For example, in this case,

00:22:21.546 --> 00:22:24.660
we come up with the relation
film language but

00:22:24.661 --> 00:22:27.800
we have an option
between two subjects.

00:22:27.801 --> 00:22:31.130
This one right here comes up

00:22:31.131 --> 00:22:32.700
four times in Freebase while

00:22:32.701 --> 00:22:34.720
this one right here
comes up in one,

00:22:34.721 --> 00:22:37.300
and that distribution allows

00:22:37.301 --> 00:22:40.030
us to set in majority
upper bound.

00:22:40.031 --> 00:22:43.100
So we've picked the subject
relations which happened most

00:22:43.101 --> 00:22:46.245
often in Freebase and we find
that the best we can do,

00:22:46.246 --> 00:22:48.960
if we follow that simple baseline

00:22:48.961 --> 00:22:51.775
is 83.4 percent on this dataset,

00:22:51.776 --> 00:22:55.430
which is quite close to
the 77 percent state of the art.

00:22:55.431 --> 00:23:00.890
Okay. Then the
second contribution

00:23:00.891 --> 00:23:03.170
is we're actually able
to get state of the art

00:23:03.171 --> 00:23:07.180
whith just standard
methods with fine-tuning.

00:23:07.340 --> 00:23:09.480
So our baseline model is going to

00:23:09.481 --> 00:23:10.995
consist of two neural models.

00:23:10.996 --> 00:23:11.960
The first one is going to be

00:23:11.961 --> 00:23:14.170
Top-K Subject Recognition which

00:23:14.171 --> 00:23:16.150
models the distribution of

00:23:16.151 --> 00:23:18.515
spans condition on the question.

00:23:18.516 --> 00:23:19.870
Secondly, we're going to have

00:23:19.871 --> 00:23:22.090
a Relation Classification model

00:23:22.091 --> 00:23:26.925
conditioned on the question
and span modeling relation.

00:23:26.926 --> 00:23:30.110
So, to better understand those,

00:23:30.111 --> 00:23:32.270
here's just an input and output.

00:23:32.271 --> 00:23:36.560
So we want film story by to have

00:23:36.561 --> 00:23:41.710
the large probability given
this input, same up here.

00:23:42.490 --> 00:23:46.540
So, why might we consider Top-K?

00:23:46.541 --> 00:23:49.150
Because the first predicted span

00:23:49.151 --> 00:23:50.815
might not always be helpful,

00:23:50.816 --> 00:23:52.470
it might not always
be in Freebase.

00:23:52.471 --> 00:23:54.580
So we can do a little bit
better by just

00:23:54.581 --> 00:23:58.730
filtering what is in
Freebase and what is not.

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:05.140
Some more details with
this standards CRF Tagger.

00:24:05.141 --> 00:24:07.110
We use viterbi inference which is

00:24:07.111 --> 00:24:09.770
standard with a CRF Tagger.

00:24:09.771 --> 00:24:11.835
We use IO Tagging,

00:24:11.836 --> 00:24:13.185
GloVe embeddings,

00:24:13.186 --> 00:24:16.290
and Grid search for
hyperparameter optimization.

00:24:16.291 --> 00:24:21.955
Similarly here, we use a BiLSTM
of batchnorm and softmax.

00:24:21.956 --> 00:24:24.600
We use AMSGrad which is

00:24:24.601 --> 00:24:27.320
the new version of Adam
published recently.

00:24:27.321 --> 00:24:31.465
FastText and Hyperband for
hyperparameter optimization.

00:24:31.466 --> 00:24:35.930
So the results.

00:24:35.931 --> 00:24:38.360
So these are the past results,

00:24:38.361 --> 00:24:40.615
a lot of research has
been done on this

00:24:40.616 --> 00:24:43.115
and boards that all
introduce this paper,

00:24:43.116 --> 00:24:45.520
or introduce this dataset.

00:24:45.521 --> 00:24:47.320
One sec.

00:24:47.321 --> 00:24:52.590
To give you a little bit
of history here,

00:24:53.560 --> 00:24:55.980
the first work done on this was

00:24:55.981 --> 00:24:58.140
primarily trying to do
an end-to-end method.

00:24:58.141 --> 00:25:00.370
So looking at one model
that can both

00:25:00.371 --> 00:25:03.530
do relation and subject
classification.

00:25:04.660 --> 00:25:09.580
These next two,
more recent papers,

00:25:09.581 --> 00:25:12.285
we're looking at baseline
approaches of two models

00:25:12.286 --> 00:25:14.980
and then these last three papers
were all building

00:25:14.981 --> 00:25:17.880
custom architectures with
three models at times,

00:25:17.881 --> 00:25:21.780
for various reasons.

00:25:21.781 --> 00:25:23.895
So then applying this
standard approach

00:25:23.896 --> 00:25:28.320
in better understanding
the dataset with fine-tuning,

00:25:28.321 --> 00:25:31.010
we're able to get 78.1 percent,

00:25:31.011 --> 00:25:34.040
which is close to
our upper bound of 83.4 percent.

00:25:34.041 --> 00:25:35.990
So finally, we want to

00:25:35.991 --> 00:25:38.705
understand what is
the difference here.

00:25:38.706 --> 00:25:41.740
What work is left to be done?

00:25:43.350 --> 00:25:45.500
So in understanding this,

00:25:45.501 --> 00:25:48.615
we first consider
our first contribution

00:25:48.616 --> 00:25:51.245
and we allow for

00:25:51.246 --> 00:25:54.000
all correct interpretations
to be correct,

00:25:54.001 --> 00:25:59.035
and that is 13 percent
of our error,

00:25:59.036 --> 00:26:01.070
to start with, then we

00:26:01.071 --> 00:26:03.670
found there is
a little bit of noise.

00:26:03.671 --> 00:26:06.555
Finally, for the remaining error,

00:26:06.556 --> 00:26:09.480
we found out that there's
actually further ambiguity,

00:26:09.481 --> 00:26:12.685
so our initial analysis
was very static.

00:26:12.686 --> 00:26:17.080
So, if there's any small changes
in the sentence,

00:26:17.081 --> 00:26:21.320
this doesn't catch, that
didn't catch that ambiguity.

00:26:21.321 --> 00:26:23.930
So empirical analysis show
that upper bound is

00:26:23.931 --> 00:26:26.920
actually loose due to
this further ambiguity.

00:26:26.921 --> 00:26:32.110
We find that in
real model errors,

00:26:32.111 --> 00:26:36.170
that most of the work
left on this dataset

00:26:36.171 --> 00:26:38.230
is actually in
low short relations

00:26:38.231 --> 00:26:40.100
where we have
less than ten examples.

00:26:40.101 --> 00:26:46.340
So in concluding,

00:26:46.341 --> 00:26:49.230
due to the first ambiguity

00:26:49.231 --> 00:26:51.625
found and due to
the further ambiguity we found,

00:26:51.626 --> 00:26:54.150
there's not much more than
four percent performance

00:26:54.151 --> 00:26:55.830
left on this dataset, we believe.

00:26:55.831 --> 00:27:00.610
Secondly, we believe that

00:27:00.611 --> 00:27:03.105
simple questions task
is largely solved

00:27:03.106 --> 00:27:07.290
and this is due to the fact that

00:27:07.291 --> 00:27:12.155
the remaining errors are
mostly due to a lack of data,

00:27:12.156 --> 00:27:14.430
which is interesting
a little bit.

00:27:14.431 --> 00:27:24.120
Yeah. Alright. Thank you.

00:27:24.121 --> 00:27:25.620
&gt;&gt; I guess we can take questions,

00:27:25.621 --> 00:27:27.130
we have a lot of time.

00:27:27.131 --> 00:27:28.580
&gt;&gt; Yeah, go ahead.

00:27:28.581 --> 00:27:34.050
&gt;&gt; If the upper bound is that
or it sounds like there's

00:27:34.051 --> 00:27:36.380
the good reason to say
maybe we shouldn't just

00:27:36.381 --> 00:27:39.120
be returning the top
best answer, right?

00:27:39.121 --> 00:27:40.400
Maybe it should actually be

00:27:40.401 --> 00:27:41.990
the best two or three of this,

00:27:41.991 --> 00:27:44.760
especially if this
is to used by user.

00:27:44.761 --> 00:27:46.340
I understand why
you'd want the top

00:27:46.341 --> 00:27:48.020
one to compared to other systems.

00:27:48.021 --> 00:27:50.180
But, have you
considered maybe making

00:27:50.181 --> 00:27:52.760
an argument to for share tasks,

00:27:52.761 --> 00:27:54.110
like maybe we want
to look at the top

00:27:54.111 --> 00:27:57.795
three best answers or
something like that.

00:27:57.796 --> 00:28:00.080
&gt;&gt; Yeah. For future datasets,

00:28:00.081 --> 00:28:02.440
it's definitely important
to acknowledge the issue

00:28:02.441 --> 00:28:05.075
that sometimes
the top one answer,

00:28:05.076 --> 00:28:10.545
there could be multiple
correct interpretations. Yeah.

00:28:10.546 --> 00:28:13.140
&gt;&gt; I have one question
here. You mentioned

00:28:13.141 --> 00:28:15.550
that you could have
trained this end-to-end,

00:28:15.551 --> 00:28:19.505
the relation classifier
and the other classifier.

00:28:19.506 --> 00:28:21.875
Can you elaborate on
why you didn't do that?

00:28:21.876 --> 00:28:23.880
If you had done it,
would you have solved

00:28:23.881 --> 00:28:26.810
some of the issues that
you were mentioning?

00:28:29.030 --> 00:28:32.215
&gt;&gt; So, fundamentally,

00:28:32.216 --> 00:28:34.400
the purpose or our work

00:28:34.401 --> 00:28:37.000
was trying to be
baseline and standard.

00:28:37.001 --> 00:28:41.410
So, to integrate both tagging and

00:28:41.411 --> 00:28:43.275
classification in one model

00:28:43.276 --> 00:28:46.340
requires more than baseline work.

00:28:46.341 --> 00:28:50.200
So, this is just the paper
first setting the baseline on

00:28:50.201 --> 00:28:52.300
this task before thinking

00:28:52.301 --> 00:28:55.200
about more custom architectures.

00:28:55.201 --> 00:28:58.130
&gt;&gt; Do you have any examples
that you can show us

00:28:58.131 --> 00:29:00.810
today? Did you prepare any?

00:29:00.811 --> 00:29:02.045
&gt;&gt; Of custom architectures?

00:29:02.046 --> 00:29:03.060
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:29:03.061 --> 00:29:03.690
&gt;&gt; No.

00:29:03.691 --> 00:29:09.880
&gt;&gt; We'd like to question some
errors that the model made.

00:29:10.790 --> 00:29:12.240
&gt;&gt; I don't have.

00:29:12.241 --> 00:29:13.635
&gt;&gt; It would have been easier for,

00:29:13.636 --> 00:29:15.760
I think you showed a few earlier,

00:29:15.761 --> 00:29:18.590
but that's fine. If you
don't have it, that's fine.

00:29:18.591 --> 00:29:20.810
&gt;&gt; Okay. Are you talking
about these errors?

00:29:20.811 --> 00:29:21.760
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:29:21.761 --> 00:29:24.060
&gt;&gt; Okay. I can send
them to you later.

00:29:24.061 --> 00:29:28.775
&gt;&gt; Yeah. No problem. It
would be nice for us to see

00:29:28.776 --> 00:29:31.190
what kind of errors that you're

00:29:31.191 --> 00:29:34.445
missing, false positives, maybe.

00:29:34.446 --> 00:29:38.870
&gt;&gt; Yeah, okay.

00:29:38.871 --> 00:29:40.020
&gt;&gt; I don't know why I'm
not getting it. Does this

00:29:40.021 --> 00:29:41.400
mean this is not really

00:29:41.401 --> 00:29:44.000
a perfect simple questions
archives because there are

00:29:44.001 --> 00:29:47.450
multiple answers to the
questions that are listed there?

00:29:47.451 --> 00:29:49.925
Should you fix the test set?

00:29:49.926 --> 00:29:54.940
So, is this all simple questions?

00:29:54.941 --> 00:29:57.905
&gt;&gt; It's already
all simple questions.

00:29:57.906 --> 00:30:02.980
Just these simple questions tend

00:30:02.981 --> 00:30:05.180
to contradict
themselves with regards

00:30:05.181 --> 00:30:07.770
to which relation
they're looking at.

00:30:07.771 --> 00:30:11.270
For example, if we're
talking about the book,

00:30:11.271 --> 00:30:13.210
Gulliver's Travels, we might

00:30:13.211 --> 00:30:15.390
ask what is the language
of Gulliver's Travels?

00:30:15.391 --> 00:30:17.720
If we're talking about
the film Gulliver's Travels,

00:30:17.721 --> 00:30:19.520
we might also ask what

00:30:19.521 --> 00:30:21.715
is the language of
Gulliver's Travels?

00:30:21.716 --> 00:30:24.210
It's not clear if you're
talking about the book

00:30:24.211 --> 00:30:27.060
or film unless you integrate

00:30:27.061 --> 00:30:34.250
more complexity into
that question or, yeah.

00:30:34.251 --> 00:30:36.215
&gt;&gt; That's an ambiguous answer,

00:30:36.216 --> 00:30:38.725
it's still a simple question?

00:30:38.726 --> 00:30:44.490
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Simple question can
have an ambiguous. Yeah.

00:30:46.690 --> 00:30:50.670
&gt;&gt; Okay. Let's thank
Michael again.

00:30:53.160 --> 00:30:59.050
So, we have Yi from
[inaudible] , Yi Luan.

00:30:59.051 --> 00:31:00.160
I guess you can introduce

00:31:00.161 --> 00:31:02.580
[inaudible] for yourself.
Take it away. Thanks.

00:31:02.581 --> 00:31:04.270
&gt;&gt; Hi everyone, I'm Yi.

00:31:04.271 --> 00:31:05.940
I'm working with Mari and Hannah.

00:31:05.941 --> 00:31:08.900
I'm going to present
our recent work about

00:31:08.901 --> 00:31:10.880
Scientific Relation
Extraction with

00:31:10.881 --> 00:31:13.450
selectively incorporated
concept embeddings.

00:31:13.451 --> 00:31:17.260
So, the reason we

00:31:17.261 --> 00:31:18.700
are doing this work is that there

00:31:18.701 --> 00:31:20.420
are countless scientific papers

00:31:20.421 --> 00:31:21.945
published each year and

00:31:21.946 --> 00:31:24.780
the tools that can

00:31:24.781 --> 00:31:27.860
manage those scientific
knowledge are in great demand.

00:31:27.861 --> 00:31:33.840
So, the goal for
this research is that we want

00:31:33.841 --> 00:31:36.930
to develop information
extraction tools

00:31:36.931 --> 00:31:38.960
using NLP approaches that

00:31:38.961 --> 00:31:41.210
can support
scientific literature.

00:31:41.211 --> 00:31:44.375
So basically, we want to build

00:31:44.376 --> 00:31:47.120
scientific knowledge graph
and also we want to

00:31:47.121 --> 00:31:49.910
answer some specific
scientific search queries.

00:31:49.911 --> 00:31:51.930
So for example, if
you ask the computer,

00:31:51.931 --> 00:31:54.185
"Show me the methods for
Apollo speech tagging,"

00:31:54.186 --> 00:31:56.840
then the computer can
give us a list of

00:31:56.841 --> 00:31:59.050
recommendations of methods that

00:31:59.051 --> 00:32:00.520
can solve the problem such

00:32:00.521 --> 00:32:06.450
as [inaudible] and HMM.

00:32:06.451 --> 00:32:08.280
There are basically
two major steps

00:32:08.281 --> 00:32:12.165
towards the task of scientific
information extraction.

00:32:12.166 --> 00:32:13.350
So, the first one is

00:32:13.351 --> 00:32:16.790
scientific concept
extraction which

00:32:16.791 --> 00:32:20.470
is first introduced
in SemEval 2017.

00:32:20.471 --> 00:32:24.430
So, the task will be to
identify some scientific phrase

00:32:24.431 --> 00:32:25.960
spans and also classify

00:32:25.961 --> 00:32:28.210
the phrase span into
concept categories,

00:32:28.211 --> 00:32:30.920
such as method,
task and material.

00:32:30.921 --> 00:32:33.295
So, for example in this sentence,

00:32:33.296 --> 00:32:35.225
Conditional Random Fields and

00:32:35.226 --> 00:32:36.520
Named Entity Recognition will

00:32:36.521 --> 00:32:40.520
be extracted and classified
as Method and Task.

00:32:40.521 --> 00:32:43.610
But the focus of
this paper will be

00:32:43.611 --> 00:32:46.615
doing the Scientific
Relation Extraction,

00:32:46.616 --> 00:32:48.880
which is to classify
the relations

00:32:48.881 --> 00:32:52.410
between the pairs of
scientific concepts.

00:32:52.411 --> 00:32:54.670
So, for example,
in this sentence,

00:32:54.671 --> 00:32:56.150
we want to know that the relation

00:32:56.151 --> 00:32:57.870
between Conditional
Random Fields and Named

00:32:57.871 --> 00:33:00.260
Entity Recognition
is that one concept

00:33:00.261 --> 00:33:02.670
is used for the other one.

00:33:03.910 --> 00:33:10.635
So, the task is that given
the candidate concept pairs,

00:33:10.636 --> 00:33:12.000
we'll want to classify them

00:33:12.001 --> 00:33:14.345
into scientific relation types.

00:33:14.346 --> 00:33:18.440
So, we introduce
five asymmetric relations

00:33:18.441 --> 00:33:19.890
which is Usage,

00:33:19.891 --> 00:33:23.240
Result, Model,
Part_Of, and Topic,

00:33:23.241 --> 00:33:25.960
and one symmetric relation
which is Comparison.

00:33:25.961 --> 00:33:27.500
So, the dataset we use is

00:33:27.501 --> 00:33:30.680
SemEval 2018 Task seven which has

00:33:30.681 --> 00:33:39.050
500 annotated scientific
abstracts from [inaudible].

00:33:39.051 --> 00:33:41.320
The main challenge of
this task is that,

00:33:41.321 --> 00:33:43.180
the annotation is pretty hard

00:33:43.181 --> 00:33:46.020
which require domain expertise.

00:33:46.021 --> 00:33:47.970
So, this result seem

00:33:47.971 --> 00:33:50.670
like we only have
limited training data.

00:33:50.671 --> 00:33:52.540
So, the contribution of

00:33:52.541 --> 00:33:55.110
this research is
that we introduce

00:33:55.111 --> 00:33:57.550
state-of-the-art
Neural Relation Extraction Model

00:33:57.551 --> 00:33:59.565
to scientific domain,

00:33:59.566 --> 00:34:03.240
and also we propose a novel
concept selection layer to

00:34:03.241 --> 00:34:04.620
the neural structure to

00:34:04.621 --> 00:34:07.290
mitigate the data
scarcity problem.

00:34:07.291 --> 00:34:09.540
We obtain the
state-of-the-art results in

00:34:09.541 --> 00:34:11.470
SemEval 2018 Task seven.

00:34:11.471 --> 00:34:18.935
So, this is the overall structure

00:34:18.936 --> 00:34:21.940
of our Neural Relation
Extraction Model,

00:34:21.941 --> 00:34:24.660
which has mainly four components.

00:34:24.661 --> 00:34:28.450
So, first given, this
sequence of text,

00:34:28.451 --> 00:34:31.700
we want to use a Token
Sequential Layer to represent,

00:34:31.701 --> 00:34:35.385
to do our representation
for the sequence and then

00:34:35.386 --> 00:34:37.680
the representation
will be fit into

00:34:37.681 --> 00:34:39.140
a Concept Presentation Layer

00:34:39.141 --> 00:34:41.225
and Dependency Sequence Layer.

00:34:41.226 --> 00:34:44.670
Then those representations
will be fit into

00:34:44.671 --> 00:34:47.275
the final Relation
Classification Layer

00:34:47.276 --> 00:34:49.870
and give us the final output.

00:34:49.871 --> 00:34:56.330
First, the Token
Sequence Layer consists

00:34:56.331 --> 00:34:59.550
of bi-directional LSTM which

00:34:59.551 --> 00:35:03.505
takes the input of [inaudible]
matting and some featuring mattings.

00:35:03.506 --> 00:35:06.230
Then the Dependency
Sequential Layer

00:35:06.231 --> 00:35:10.185
is based on this representation.

00:35:10.186 --> 00:35:17.255
We pass the sentence using
some dependency parser

00:35:17.256 --> 00:35:20.880
and then we extract

00:35:21.220 --> 00:35:25.740
the shortest pass between
the two concepts.

00:35:26.130 --> 00:35:32.645
Then we decompose the dependency
pass into two parts.

00:35:32.646 --> 00:35:35.170
We first find that
the common ancestor between

00:35:35.171 --> 00:35:38.580
the two concepts
that were used in

00:35:38.581 --> 00:35:41.320
this case and then

00:35:41.970 --> 00:35:44.570
based on the direction
of the relation,

00:35:44.571 --> 00:35:46.600
we will model Forward Dependency

00:35:46.601 --> 00:35:47.890
pass and Backward Dependency

00:35:47.891 --> 00:35:52.375
pass which represents
both the concepts,

00:35:52.376 --> 00:35:55.730
the dependency passed
between the concept and

00:35:55.731 --> 00:36:00.860
the common ancestor., So,

00:36:00.861 --> 00:36:02.750
we would fit in the head

00:36:02.751 --> 00:36:05.250
word representation
of each concept

00:36:05.251 --> 00:36:07.880
and the common ancestor
representation to

00:36:07.881 --> 00:36:10.810
the Backward and
Forward Dependency pass.

00:36:10.811 --> 00:36:16.000
Then we would use
the representation,

00:36:16.001 --> 00:36:19.555
concatenate those tokens as

00:36:19.556 --> 00:36:21.910
a final representation
for the relation.

00:36:21.911 --> 00:36:26.335
Then we can output
the final result.

00:36:26.336 --> 00:36:31.450
So, this dependency presentation

00:36:31.451 --> 00:36:34.605
itself can already give
us pretty good result,

00:36:34.606 --> 00:36:37.550
but something is missing in

00:36:37.551 --> 00:36:39.440
this structure that is

00:36:39.441 --> 00:36:43.155
the representation
for the concepts.

00:36:43.156 --> 00:36:47.150
This can model the whole span
of the phrases.

00:36:47.360 --> 00:36:51.120
A traditional way
of doing this is,

00:36:51.121 --> 00:36:54.420
an example, Miwa and Bansal 2016,

00:36:54.421 --> 00:36:58.005
which they automatically
learn a weight,

00:36:58.006 --> 00:36:59.070
Alpha, for each of

00:36:59.071 --> 00:37:02.225
the individual token
in the whole concept.

00:37:02.226 --> 00:37:04.110
And then there was some over

00:37:04.111 --> 00:37:10.110
all of the tokens within
the concept span.

00:37:11.080 --> 00:37:14.290
The limitation of
this approach is that

00:37:14.291 --> 00:37:18.675
without introducing any prior
knowledge of the concept,

00:37:18.676 --> 00:37:21.030
the automatically learned
representation will

00:37:21.031 --> 00:37:25.570
generalize poorly
in the test time.

00:37:25.760 --> 00:37:28.550
So, in order to
solve that problem,

00:37:28.551 --> 00:37:30.120
we propose a new approach,

00:37:30.121 --> 00:37:31.870
which is concept selection.

00:37:31.871 --> 00:37:33.930
So, we first construct

00:37:33.931 --> 00:37:37.585
a concept dictionary using
the frequent concepts,

00:37:37.586 --> 00:37:42.885
and then learn a [inaudible]
for each of those concepts.

00:37:42.886 --> 00:37:45.140
And then give it a new concept,

00:37:45.141 --> 00:37:48.630
where we'll look up through

00:37:48.631 --> 00:37:50.550
the concept dictionary
and see if there

00:37:50.551 --> 00:37:52.845
is exact match for that phrase.

00:37:52.846 --> 00:37:55.780
So, if there is one exact
match for the phrase,

00:37:55.781 --> 00:37:57.320
we will use the pressure
and embedding

00:37:57.321 --> 00:37:59.645
directly as a concept embedding.

00:37:59.646 --> 00:38:02.275
But if there isn't
an exact match,

00:38:02.276 --> 00:38:07.880
where we decompose the phrase
into some sub n-grams,

00:38:07.881 --> 00:38:09.645
so, for example here, here,

00:38:09.646 --> 00:38:13.530
pronominal, say the candidate

00:38:13.531 --> 00:38:15.860
is pronominal
anaphora resolution,

00:38:15.861 --> 00:38:19.290
but there's no exact match
in the concept dictionary,

00:38:19.291 --> 00:38:21.475
so we will decompose

00:38:21.476 --> 00:38:28.120
the whole phrase into
bigrams and unigrams.

00:38:28.121 --> 00:38:30.590
Then we look up those bigrams and

00:38:30.591 --> 00:38:33.505
unigrams in the dictionary
and find some meshes.

00:38:33.506 --> 00:38:37.080
So, here is the resulting
candidates that we could find,

00:38:37.081 --> 00:38:39.690
which is anaphora resolution,
resolution, and anaphora.

00:38:39.691 --> 00:38:41.930
Then we will use

00:38:41.931 --> 00:38:46.525
these candidates together
with a dummy vector,

00:38:46.526 --> 00:38:51.330
the empty, to construct
the vector of candidate sets.

00:38:51.331 --> 00:38:54.830
And then, in order to learn
the final representation,

00:38:54.831 --> 00:39:00.360
we need to selectively combine
all those representations.

00:39:00.361 --> 00:39:02.950
So in that case, we use

00:39:02.951 --> 00:39:06.060
attention mechanism
to learn weight

00:39:06.061 --> 00:39:09.905
between each of these

00:39:09.906 --> 00:39:17.110
embedding and the representation
in the sequential layer.

00:39:18.250 --> 00:39:20.580
And then we will sum over

00:39:20.581 --> 00:39:24.880
all the candidates to
get the final embedding.

00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:31.060
In order to get
the prior phrase knowledge,

00:39:31.061 --> 00:39:33.220
we would first extract

00:39:33.221 --> 00:39:35.850
all the concepts in
large-scale scientific corpus

00:39:35.851 --> 00:39:38.630
using state-of-the-art
scientific term extractor

00:39:38.631 --> 00:39:41.595
proposed in 2017.

00:39:41.596 --> 00:39:45.800
Then we use Word2vec to train

00:39:45.801 --> 00:39:48.105
all the frequent
concepts and then

00:39:48.106 --> 00:39:52.470
construct this concept
dictionary.

00:39:52.471 --> 00:39:56.220
The dataset that we'll use

00:39:56.221 --> 00:40:00.360
in this task is
SemEval 2018 Task seven,

00:40:00.361 --> 00:40:02.170
which has three sub-tasks.

00:40:02.171 --> 00:40:04.900
So the first subtask is
relation classification,

00:40:04.901 --> 00:40:08.330
which we assume that

00:40:08.331 --> 00:40:10.865
concept pairs and
relation directions are given,

00:40:10.866 --> 00:40:14.270
so we need to choose between
the six defined relations.

00:40:14.271 --> 00:40:17.335
The second task is
relation extraction,

00:40:17.336 --> 00:40:19.390
where given all the concepts,

00:40:19.391 --> 00:40:21.825
we need to identify if
the relation exists,

00:40:21.826 --> 00:40:24.330
and this is the binary
classification task.

00:40:24.331 --> 00:40:25.800
The third task is

00:40:25.801 --> 00:40:28.575
relation extraction and
classification task.

00:40:28.576 --> 00:40:31.930
So, we need first to extract
those relations and then

00:40:31.931 --> 00:40:33.960
classify them into six relations

00:40:33.961 --> 00:40:36.235
together with their directions.

00:40:36.236 --> 00:40:38.400
So, the external dataset we

00:40:38.401 --> 00:40:40.320
use to train
this concept embedding

00:40:40.321 --> 00:40:43.800
is semantic scholar corpus,

00:40:43.801 --> 00:40:46.700
which has 110K abstracts,

00:40:46.701 --> 00:40:49.300
and the ACL Anthology
referenced corpus,

00:40:49.301 --> 00:40:52.950
which has 22K full papers.

00:40:53.990 --> 00:40:57.255
So, here is the results.

00:40:57.256 --> 00:41:02.444
Our system got the best result
in subtask 2.1,

00:41:02.445 --> 00:41:08.085
and we also do a good job
in other two tasks.

00:41:08.086 --> 00:41:11.920
So, Team One data a little bit
better than this,

00:41:11.921 --> 00:41:15.400
but they use
more supervised data.

00:41:16.330 --> 00:41:19.840
We also perform
ablation studies by

00:41:19.841 --> 00:41:24.200
removing some of the components
of the neural network.

00:41:35.390 --> 00:41:39.425
So, we first remove
the dependency layer,

00:41:39.426 --> 00:41:45.445
which we could see that
the F1 score decreased greatly,

00:41:45.446 --> 00:41:48.065
and then we will move
this concept layer,

00:41:48.066 --> 00:41:53.235
which also results in
a slightly worse F1 score.

00:41:53.236 --> 00:41:55.330
We also perform
the baseline method,

00:41:55.331 --> 00:41:58.300
which is the weighted sum
of the tokens,

00:41:58.301 --> 00:42:02.480
and this one also didn't
perform very well.

00:42:02.481 --> 00:42:05.070
So, the conclusion is that

00:42:05.071 --> 00:42:07.440
the dependency information
is very important

00:42:07.441 --> 00:42:09.995
in this task because we
don't have a lot of data,

00:42:09.996 --> 00:42:11.555
and some external knowledge

00:42:11.556 --> 00:42:14.445
from the dependency parser
is very important.

00:42:14.446 --> 00:42:19.395
Also, the baseline method
decrease the performance.

00:42:19.396 --> 00:42:22.425
We think this is because

00:42:22.426 --> 00:42:26.830
it introduce some
redundant parameters.

00:42:28.640 --> 00:42:33.510
We also find out that replace the
[inaudible] representation we

00:42:33.511 --> 00:42:36.300
selected between
the concept layer

00:42:36.301 --> 00:42:38.925
would significantly
improve their performance,

00:42:38.926 --> 00:42:43.405
which can give us
the best result of 44.1.

00:42:43.406 --> 00:42:47.190
So, the summary of this research
is that we introduce

00:42:47.191 --> 00:42:49.990
a new neural relation
extraction model

00:42:49.991 --> 00:42:51.205
to scientific domain,

00:42:51.206 --> 00:42:54.380
and we propose a novel
Concept Selection Layer to

00:42:54.381 --> 00:42:58.445
the neural structure that
can leverage unlabeled data.

00:42:58.446 --> 00:43:01.010
Also, we obtain
the state-of-the-art result in

00:43:01.011 --> 00:43:04.240
SemEval 2018 Task seven.

00:43:04.241 --> 00:43:07.660
So that's it. Any questions?

